---
// About page: who runs the guide, why, and how the data is gathered and ranked.
// The copy is the owner's approved text (openspec add-about-page design.md).
import { Picture, getImage } from "astro:assets";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import SeasonStamp from "../components/SeasonStamp.astro";
import JsonLd from "../components/JsonLd.astro";
import { canonicalUrl } from "../data/pages.js";
import "../styles/about.css";
import portrait from "../assets/images/about/tejen-shrestha.jpeg";

// The season "On the skin track" was written for. Update it together with that
// section's copy each fall; until then the stamp reads "Written in <season>"
// once CURRENT_SEASON (src/shared.js) moves on.
const SKIN_TRACK_SEASON = "2026-27";

// ProfilePage structured data: name and portrait only, never contact details
const PATH = "/about.html";
const schemaImage = await getImage({
  src: portrait,
  width: 400,
  format: "jpeg",
});
const profile = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "ProfilePage",
  url: canonicalUrl(PATH),
  mainEntity: {
    "@type": "Person",
    name: "Tejen Shrestha",
    image: new URL(schemaImage.src, Astro.site).href,
  },
};
---

<Base
  path={PATH}
  title="About Tejen Shrestha | Uphill Ski Colorado"
  description="Why Tejen Shrestha built Uphill Ski Colorado, how the guide's uphill data is gathered from official resort websites, and how the resorts are ranked."
  keywords="about uphill ski colorado, Tejen Shrestha, uphill skiing guide, Colorado ski touring"
  imageAlt="About - Uphill Ski Colorado"
  appTitle="About"
  heroClass="about-hero"
  current="about"
>
  <JsonLd slot="head" data={profile} />
  <section class="about-hero" aria-labelledby="page-title">
    <div class="about-hero-inner">
      <Picture
        class="about-portrait"
        src={portrait}
        formats={["avif", "webp"]}
        fallbackFormat="jpeg"
        widths={[160, 200, 320, 400]}
        sizes="(min-width: 700px) 200px, 160px"
        width={200}
        height={200}
        loading="eager"
        fetchpriority="high"
        alt="Tejen Shrestha, smiling in the sun"
      />
      <div class="about-id">
        <h1 class="about-name" id="page-title">
          Tejen Shrestha
        </h1>
        <p class="about-intro">
          Software developer in Denver, uphill skier, and the person who keeps
          this guide up to date.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <svg
      class="about-edge"
      viewBox="0 0 1000 80"
      preserveAspectRatio="none"
      aria-hidden="true"
    >
      <path d="M0 13L18 31L31 12L44 27L78 42L89 19L102 37L137 29L150 8L164 22L204 59L218 59L233 62L269 35L280 11L291 33L328 35L342 19L358 30L389 56L398 49L406 51L440 49L455 34L472 47L512 61L524 55L538 59L569 38L579 19L589 37L623 60L637 57L652 57L684 56L693 55L702 60L733 40L746 16L760 34L795 32L807 8L820 29L851 37L860 27L870 34L902 33L914 19L928 34L964 55L977 57L991 55L1000 80L0 80Z"></path>
    </svg>
  </section>

  <main id="main-content" class="about-main">
    <section class="about-section" aria-labelledby="why-i-built-this">
      <div class="about-prose">
        <h2 id="why-i-built-this">Why I built this</h2>
        <p>
          It started as a way to get into the mountains without buying an
          expensive lift pass. I knew about backcountry skiing, but I had no
          idea you could skin uphill at resorts. I found out by accident while
          browsing Winter Park’s trail maps, when I spotted a dedicated uphill
          route map.
        </p>
        <p>
          I dug in and learned you could skin up Winter Park all day, every day,
          for $25 a season. I was stoked. I went straight to Marketplace to find
          a used splitboard, and uphilling made me fall in love with Winter
          Park. It’s such a wonderful winter playground.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then I got curious about the other resorts. I spent a lot of time
          poring over trail maps and planning weekend routes. I’m a software
          developer by day and I’ve always liked building things, so this turned
          into a side project I hoped other splitboarders and skiers would find
          useful.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section" aria-labelledby="about-me">
      <div class="about-prose">
        <h2 id="about-me">About me</h2>
        <p>
          I was born in Kathmandu, Nepal, and grew up in awe of the Himalayas.
          When I was twelve, my family moved to Silver Spring, Maryland. There
          are no mountains there, and I missed them a lot. A couple of decades
          later, climbing and mountain biking brought my love of the outdoors
          back.
        </p>
        <p>
          In 2022, my wife and I moved to Denver, and the mountains have been my
          playground ever since. I’ve gotten really into trail running in the
          mountains, gravel riding and scrambling the Flatirons. In 2025 I
          finished the Leadville Heavy Half, the GoPro Mountain Games 20K and
          the Boulderthon marathon. This year’s race was the 10 Seconds Flat:
          ten laps of the Second Flatiron, an unofficial backyard race among
          friends. Whatever the season, my favourite thing is being up high and
          finding a good traverse.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="about-card">
        <p class="about-card-label" id="backyard-favourites">
          Backyard favourites
        </p>
        <ul aria-labelledby="backyard-favourites">
          <li>The First, Second and Third Flatirons</li>
          <li>Green Mountain, Bear Peak and South Boulder Peak</li>
          <li>Staunton State Park</li>
          <li>Apex Park</li>
          <li>The Tenmile Range above Frisco</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section" aria-labelledby="on-the-skin-track">
      <div class="about-prose">
        <h2 id="on-the-skin-track">On the skin track</h2>
        <SeasonStamp season={SKIN_TRACK_SEASON} kind="written" />
        <p>
          My first uphill season was 2024–25, on a splitboard. For 2025–26 I
          switched to skis. The first few outings were rough, but after a few
          lift-served days it clicked. I got in 19 uphill days and about 5
          downhill days, more than the season before, even though uphill opened
          about a month late at Winter Park.
        </p>
        <p>
          I still feel incredibly lucky to have places like Winter Park to play
          in all winter. Skinning to the top of Pano for the first time was a
          genuine “wow.”
        </p>
        <p>
          This season I’ve already been up A-Basin, a new one for my uphill
          list, and I’ve signed up for the Aspen Power of Four, so there’s a lot
          of uphill training ahead.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="about-card">
        <p class="about-card-label" id="my-setup">
          My setup
        </p>
        <ul aria-labelledby="my-setup">
          <li>
            <span class="about-card-key">Skis:</span> Atomic Backland UL 86
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="about-card-key">Boots:</span> Scarpa F1 LT
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section" aria-labelledby="how-this-guide-works">
      <div class="about-prose">
        <h2 id="how-this-guide-works">How this guide works</h2>
        <p>
          Every policy here comes from the resort’s own official website. Uphill
          details come out gradually through the fall and change during the
          winter, so I update the guide several times a season. Code I wrote for
          this site does most of the gathering, but I check everything by hand
          against each resort’s official page.
        </p>
        <p>
          The ranking weighs ease of access, hours, cost, and how much ground
          you can cover. To be honest, Winter Park being #1 is partly personal
          bias. It’s where I started skiing, and the ski train lets me skip the
          I-70 madness. But I also think it has the best price and policy, and a
          lot of mountain you can cover on skins.
        </p>
        <p>
          I wish more resorts followed Winter Park’s lead. Several of the big
          resorts, especially on the Epic side, only allow uphill before and
          after lift hours. Maybe that’s for insurance reasons, but I think
          opening up uphill access would bring them more visitors and more
          business.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>

<script>
  import "../about.js";
</script>
